#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:1
msgid "Language Basics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:3
msgid ""
"This page introduces the core TileLang (tile‑lang) DSL that you’ll use to "
"write high‑performance kernels. It focuses on how to define a kernel, "
"express iteration, move data across memory scopes, and run it with JIT."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:7
msgid "The examples use the conventional aliases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:9
msgid ""
"import tilelang\n"
"import tilelang.language as T\n"
"from tilelang import jit\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:15
msgid "1. Defining a Kernel with `@T.prim_func`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:17
msgid ""
"TileLang kernels are TIR (TVM IR) functions produced by the `@T.prim_func` "
"decorator. Arguments are annotated with shapes and dtypes via `T.Tensor` or "
"`T.Buffer`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:21
msgid "Note on dtypes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:22
msgid ""
"You can pass dtypes as a string (e.g., 'float32'), a TileLang dtype (e.g., "
"`T.float32`), or a framework dtype (e.g., `torch.float32`). TileLang "
"normalizes all of these. See Type System for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:26
msgid ""
"@T.prim_func\n"
"def add_kernel(\n"
"    A: T.Tensor((N,), dtype),  # dtype could be 'float32' | T.float32 | "
"torch.float32\n"
"    B: T.Tensor((N,), dtype),\n"
"    C: T.Tensor((N,), dtype),\n"
"): ...  # kernel body\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:35
msgid ""
"Shapes may be concrete integers or symbolic. For symbolic, you can pass "
"Python ints through the outer `@jit` wrapper (shown below), or annotate with "
"`T.dyn` when you want a named symbolic dimension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:39
msgid ""
"# Named symbolic dimension (optional)\n"
"K = T.dyn[\"K\"]\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@T.prim_func\n"
"def uses_dyn(A: T.Tensor((K,), \"float32\")): ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:48
msgid "Dynamic symbolic dimensions: two ways"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:50
msgid ""
"TileLang supports two complementary ways to introduce symbolic (dynamic) "
"dims:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:52
msgid ""
"Type-level annotations via `T.dyn[...]` (recommended for function signatures)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:53
msgid ""
"Use in `T.Tensor((T.dyn['K'], ...), dtype)` or bind once then reuse (as "
"above)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:54
msgid ""
"Inside the kernel body, prefer reading from the buffer’s shape, e.g. `M = "
"A.shape[0]`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:56
msgid "Term-level variables via `T.dynamic(name, dtype)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:57
msgid "Creates a TIR `tir.Var` you can use directly in expressions/loops."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:58
msgid "Handy when you need to reference the dimension symbol in the body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:60
msgid ""
"# 1) Annotation-only symbol; read the bound size via shape\n"
"K = T.dyn[\"K\"]  # dtype defaults to int32\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@T.prim_func\n"
"def foo(A: T.Tensor((K,), \"float32\")):\n"
"    N = A.shape[0]\n"
"    for i in T.serial(N):\n"
"        ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"# 2) Explicit Var symbol usable in the body\n"
"K = T.dynamic(\"K\", \"int32\")  # or T.dynamic('K') defaults to int32\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@T.prim_func\n"
"def bar(A: T.Tensor((K,), \"float32\")):\n"
"    for i in T.serial(K):\n"
"        ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:82
#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:255
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:83
msgid ""
"`T.symbolic(name, dtype)` is a deprecated alias of `T.dynamic` and will be "
"removed in v0.1.15; prefer `T.dynamic`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:84
msgid ""
"Under `@jit`, concrete sizes come from the actual tensor arguments at the "
"first call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:85
msgid ""
"Symbols in annotations do not need to be separate kernel arguments; TileLang "
"binds them from argument shapes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:87
msgid "2. Launching Work with `T.Kernel`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:89
msgid ""
"`with T.Kernel(...)` declares a grid of tile programs. The positional "
"arguments give the grid extent along each axis and the returned variables "
"are the program indices along those axes. This is the part of a launch every "
"target shares: on CUDA a program is a thread block and `bx`/`by` are "
"`blockIdx.x`/`blockIdx.y`; on CPU the grid becomes the outer loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:95
msgid ""
"with T.Kernel(grid_x, grid_y, threads=128) as (bx, by):\n"
"    ...  # bx/by are the program indices (blockIdx.x/y on CUDA)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:100
msgid ""
"Keyword arguments are launch annotations that the backend interprets once "
"the target is known. Each language dialect's `Kernel` declares the "
"annotations its backend understands as explicit keyword parameters, so "
"hovering or autocompleting `T.Kernel` shows exactly those and anything else "
"is rejected: `tilelang.language` (the CUDA dialect) offers `threads`, "
"`prelude`, `cluster_dims` and `name`; `tilelang.rocm.language` / "
"`tilelang.metal.language` offer `threads` and `prelude`; "
"`tilelang.cpu.language` offers only `prelude`. `threads` is the SIMT one: "
"how many threads run each tile program on GPU-style backends. Those backends "
"pick a default (128) when it is omitted; a kernel written with the CUDA "
"dialect still compiles for CPU, which ignores the thread count. Code inside "
"`T.Kernel` operates at the tile-program level, so you rarely need raw thread "
"indices; most kernels use structured loops (`T.serial`, `T.unroll`, "
"`T.Parallel`, `T.Pipelined`) that the compiler maps onto threads. "
"`T.get_thread_binding()` exposes the thread index for thread-level code on "
"SIMT targets; a kernel that uses it is rejected when compiled for a target "
"without SIMT threads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:117
msgid ""
"`T.ClusterKernel(..., cluster_dims=...)` adds the CUDA thread-block-cluster "
"annotation (SM90+). A cluster is a `cluster_dims`-shaped tile of the grid, "
"so `T.get_cluster_id(axis)` is plain program-index arithmetic (`bx // "
"cluster_dims[axis]`) and works on every target, while "
"`T.block_rank_in_cluster()` reads the hardware rank and is CUDA-only. "
"Targets without clusters reject `cluster_dims` at compile time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:124
msgid "Naming CUDA kernels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:126
msgid ""
"Use `name=` to distinguish individual CUDA launches in generated code and "
"profilers, including multiple launches inside one `PrimFunc`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:129
msgid ""
"@T.prim_func\n"
"def pipeline(A: T.Tensor((256,), \"float32\"), B: T.Tensor((256,), "
"\"float32\")):\n"
"    with T.Kernel(2, threads=128, name=\"add_one\") as bx:\n"
"        for i in T.Parallel(128):\n"
"            B[bx * 128 + i] = A[bx * 128 + i] + 1.0\n"
"\n"
"    with T.Kernel(2, threads=128, name=\"double_values\") as bx:\n"
"        for i in T.Parallel(128):\n"
"            B[bx * 128 + i] *= 2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:141
msgid ""
"The device functions are named `add_one` and `double_values`, without an "
"extra `_kernel` suffix. Names must be non-empty ASCII identifiers matching "
"`[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`. Avoid C++ keywords and names reserved by CUDA. "
"When a symbol is already in use, TileLang appends a numeric suffix such as "
"`stage_1`. This also handles collisions with the enclosing host function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:147
msgid ""
"Omitting `name` (or passing `None`) retains the existing naming scheme based "
"on the enclosing function's `global_symbol`, such as `pipeline_kernel` and "
"`pipeline_kernel_1`. Named and unnamed launches can be mixed, and "
"`T.ClusterKernel` accepts the same `name` argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:152
msgid "3. Loops and Control Flow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:154
msgid "Core loop constructs map to familiar hardware patterns:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:156
msgid "`T.serial(start, stop[, step])`: plain for‑loop"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:157
msgid "`T.unroll(start, stop[, step])`: unrolled loop"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:158
msgid ""
"`T.Parallel(ext0, ext1, ...)`: nested parallel loops (elementwise‑friendly)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:159
msgid ""
"`T.Pipelined(iters, num_stages=N)`: software pipelining for producer/consumer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:161
msgid ""
"for i in T.serial(N):\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"for i, j in T.Parallel(M, N):\n"
"    C[i, j] = A[i, j] + B[i, j]\n"
"\n"
"for k in T.Pipelined(T.ceildiv(K, BK), num_stages=3):\n"
"    # overlap copy/compute across stages\n"
"    ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:173
msgid ""
"Conditionals use standard Python `if`/`else`. Guard edges with predicates "
"when tile sizes do not divide problem sizes evenly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:176
msgid "4. Memory Scopes and Allocation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:178
msgid "TileLang exposes key software‑managed scopes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:180
msgid "Global: device memory (default for `T.Tensor` arguments)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:181
msgid "Shared: on‑chip, block‑visible (`T.alloc_shared(shape, dtype)`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:182
msgid ""
"Fragment and scalars: per‑thread fragments and scalar vars but in Shared "
"View (`T.alloc_fragment`, `T.alloc_var`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:185
msgid ""
"A_shared = T.alloc_shared((BM, BK), \"float16\")\n"
"B_shared = T.alloc_shared((BK, BN), \"float16\")\n"
"C_local = T.alloc_fragment((BM, BN), \"float32\")\n"
"T.clear(C_local)  # zero accumulators\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:192
msgid "5. Moving Data: `T.copy`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:194
msgid ""
"Use `T.copy(src, dst)` to move tiles between scopes. It accepts buffers, "
"buffer regions, or buffer loads; extents are inferred or can be broadcast."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:197
msgid ""
"# Global -> Shared (tile copy), extents inferred from dst\n"
"T.copy(A[by * BM, ko * BK], A_shared)\n"
"T.copy(B[ko * BK, bx * BN], B_shared)\n"
"\n"
"# Fragment -> Global (store back)\n"
"T.copy(C_local, C[by * BM, bx * BN])\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:206
msgid ""
"`T.copy` performs coalescing and scope‑specific lowering during compilation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:208
msgid ""
"If you need explicitly asynchronous global->shared prefetch for manual "
"pipelining, use `T.async_copy(src, dst)`. Unlike `T.copy`, it does not auto-"
"insert any wait: you must explicitly insert `T.ptx_wait_group(...)` before "
"consuming `dst`. A shared-memory barrier is still required for cross-thread "
"consumption, but in most TileLang programs you do not need to write it "
"manually because `ThreadSync(\"shared\")` will insert the necessary "
"`T.tvm_storage_sync(\"shared\")` before the first read from `dst`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:216
msgid "6. A Minimal End‑to‑End Example (Vector Add)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:218
msgid ""
"import tilelang\n"
"import tilelang.language as T\n"
"from tilelang import jit\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@jit  # infers target from tensors at first call\n"
"def add(N: int, block: int = 256, dtype: str = \"float32\"):\n"
"\n"
"    @T.prim_func\n"
"    def add_kernel(\n"
"        A: T.Tensor((N,), dtype),\n"
"        B: T.Tensor((N,), dtype),\n"
"        C: T.Tensor((N,), dtype),\n"
"    ):\n"
"        with T.Kernel(T.ceildiv(N, block), threads=block) as bx:\n"
"            for i in T.Parallel(block):\n"
"                gi = bx * block + i\n"
"                # Optional — LegalizeSafeMemoryAccess inserts a guard when "
"an access may be OOB\n"
"                C[gi] = A[gi] + B[gi]\n"
"\n"
"    return add_kernel\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"# Host side (PyTorch shown; NumPy/DLPack also supported)\n"
"import torch\n"
"\n"
"N = 1 << 20\n"
"A = torch.randn(N, device=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.float32)\n"
"B = torch.randn(N, device=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.float32)\n"
"C = torch.empty(N, device=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.float32)\n"
"\n"
"kernel = add(N)\n"
"kernel(A, B, C)  # runs on GPU\n"
"torch.testing.assert_close(C, A + B)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:256
msgid ""
"The `@jit` wrapper returns a callable kernel after the first compilation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:257
msgid ""
"You can pass compile‑time tunables (tile sizes, dtypes) through the outer "
"Python function and bake them into the generated TIR."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:259
msgid ""
"Compile-time arguments and their defaults must be hashable. Lists, "
"dictionaries, sets, and tuples containing unhashable values raise "
"`TypeError`; use immutable, hashable values such as tuples or frozen "
"dataclasses instead. Values that affect specialization, along with their "
"hash and equality behavior, must remain stable while cached kernels are in "
"use. Runtime tensor arguments are excluded from this requirement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:266
msgid "7. Tiled GEMM Skeleton"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:268
msgid ""
"Below is a minimal pattern for a tiled GEMM using shared memory staging and "
"a fragment accumulator. It mirrors the quickstart style found in the "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:271
msgid ""
"@T.prim_func\n"
"def gemm(\n"
"    A: T.Tensor((M, K), \"float16\"),\n"
"    B: T.Tensor((K, N), \"float16\"),\n"
"    C: T.Tensor((M, N), \"float16\"),\n"
"):\n"
"    with T.Kernel(T.ceildiv(N, BN), T.ceildiv(M, BM), threads=128) as (bx, "
"by):\n"
"        A_s = T.alloc_shared((BM, BK), \"float16\")\n"
"        B_s = T.alloc_shared((BK, BN), \"float16\")\n"
"        C_f = T.alloc_fragment((BM, BN), \"float32\")\n"
"        T.clear(C_f)\n"
"\n"
"        for ko in T.Pipelined(T.ceildiv(K, BK), num_stages=3):\n"
"            T.copy(A[by * BM, ko * BK], A_s)\n"
"            T.copy(B[ko * BK, bx * BN], B_s)\n"
"            T.gemm(A_s, B_s, C_f)  # lowered to tensor‑core/ISA specific "
"kernels\n"
"\n"
"        T.copy(C_f, C[by * BM, bx * BN])\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:292
msgid "8. Debugging and Printing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:294
msgid ""
"Use `T.print` inside a kernel for quick introspection. TileLang emits "
"printing from a single thread for shared/fragment scopes to avoid floods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:297
msgid ""
"T.print(C_f, msg=\"accumulator:\")\n"
"T.print(A_s, msg=\"A tile:\")\n"
"T.print(C[0], msg=\"C[0] = \")\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:303
msgid "Configuring the Z3 proof budget"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:305
msgid ""
"Use `pass_configs` to change the Z3 resource budget for one kernel "
"compilation. The default is `100_000`; a larger budget can prove more "
"difficult expressions but may increase compilation time. The budget counts "
"Z3 resource units, not milliseconds or an exact number of search iterations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:310
msgid ""
"Both styles of `@tilelang.jit` accept the same option. In the lazy style, "
"the decorated factory returns a `PrimFunc`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:313
msgid ""
"@tilelang.jit(pass_configs={\"arith.z3_rlimit\": 100_000})\n"
"def get_kernel(N):\n"
"    @T.prim_func\n"
"    def kernel(A: T.Tensor((N,), T.float32)):\n"
"        with T.Kernel(T.ceildiv(N, 128), threads=128) as bx:\n"
"            for i in T.Parallel(128):\n"
"                if bx * 128 + i < N:\n"
"                    A[bx * 128 + i] += 1\n"
"\n"
"    return kernel\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:326
msgid ""
"In the eager style, the decorated function contains the kernel body directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:328
msgid ""
"@tilelang.jit(pass_configs={\"arith.z3_rlimit\": 100_000})\n"
"def increment(A):\n"
"    A: T.Tensor((128,), T.float32)\n"
"    with T.Kernel(1, threads=128):\n"
"        for i in T.Parallel(128):\n"
"            A[i] += 1\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:337
msgid "Direct compilation also accepts the option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:339
msgid ""
"compiled = tilelang.compile(program, pass_configs={\"arith.z3_rlimit\": "
"100_000})\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:343
msgid ""
"The equivalent enum key is `tilelang.PassConfigKey.ARITH_Z3_RLIMIT`. Values "
"must be integers from `0` through `2**32 - 1`; `0` disables the resource "
"limit. For `tilelang.lower` or direct analyzer use, set the option in "
"`tvm.transform.PassContext(config={\"arith.z3_rlimit\": 100_000})`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/language_basics.md:348
msgid ""
"Each analyzer captures the active setting when it is constructed. Z3 "
"contexts and solvers are still created lazily, and clones retain their "
"source analyzer's setting. An explicit `analyzer.set_z3_rlimit(...)` "
"overrides that captured value. The setting applies to solver checks, not to "
"a total budget across the kernel."
msgstr ""

